{"task_id": "Mbpp/125", "solution": "def find_length(binary_string: str) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the maximum difference between the number of 0s and 1s in any substring\n    of the given binary string using a Kadane's algorithm variant.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not binary_string:\n        return 0\n\n    # Validate binary string characters\n    for char in binary_string:\n        if char not in ('0', '1'):\n            raise ValueError(\"Input string must contain only '0's and '1's.\")\n\n    max_so_far = 0\n    current_max = 0\n\n    # Transform '0' to 1 and '1' to -1, then apply Kadane's algorithm\n    for char in binary_string:\n        val = 1 if char == '0' else -1\n        current_max += val\n        if current_max < 0:\n            current_max = 0\n        if current_max > max_so_far:\n            max_so_far = current_max\n            \n    # If all characters are '1's, max_so_far will be 0. We need to return -1 in such case if \n    # the question implies 'difference must be positive'. Since the example `11000010001` leads to 6,\n    # it implies that 0s - 1s, and a substring with all 1s would lead to a negative difference\n    # or 0 if empty. But in general, if the string has no '0's, the max difference (0s - 1s) would be \n    # negative, meaning the maximum difference is actually 0 (for an empty substring or no '0's).\n    # However, the example shows 6, indicating we are looking for the max difference (0s-1s) where 0s must be more than 1s to get a positive value.\n    # If the original string contains at least one '0', max_so_far will be positive or zero.\n    # If the string contains only '1's, current_max will continuously decrease, and max_so_far will remain 0.\n    # The problem implies finding max (0s - 1s) in a substring. If all are '1's, this difference is always negative.\n    # The output 6 from '11000010001' (00001000 -> 8 zeros, 1 one -> 7. Or 0000 -> 4 zeros, 0 ones -> 4. For 00001000, 8-1 = 7. Ah, (0s-1s).\n    # The example substring 00001000 has 8 zeros and 1 one. 8 - 1 = 7.\n    # What about '000000' (6 zeros, 0 ones) from '1100000010001'?\n    # It seems the problem wants the maximum positive difference.\n    # If a string contains only '1's, the max difference (0s - 1s) would be negative. \n    # Kadane's algorithm with '0' as +1 and '1' as -1 correctly handles this.\n    # If max_so_far remains 0 and there are no '0's, then the result should correctly be 0.\n    # The transformation makes sense for finding max (0s - 1s) because (num_0s * 1 + num_1s * -1)\n    # is exactly (num_0s - num_1s).\n    # For \"111\", the transformed array is [-1, -1, -1]. current_max always resets to 0. max_so_far remains 0. Correct for this interpretation.\n    \n    return max_so_far"}
