{"task_id": "Mbpp/239", "solution": "def get_total_number_of_sequences(m: int, n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the total number of possible sequences of length n,\n    where each element is a positive integer, is greater than or equal to twice the previous element,\n    and is less than or equal to m.\n    \"\"\"\n    # dp[length][last_val] stores the number of valid sequences of 'length'\n    # where the last element is 'last_val'.\n    dp = [[0] * (m + 1) for _ in range(n + 1)]\n\n    # Base case: Sequences of length 1\n    # Any positive integer from 1 to m can be a sequence of length 1.\n    for j in range(1, m + 1):\n        dp[1][j] = 1\n\n    # Fill the DP table for lengths from 2 to n\n    for length in range(2, n + 1):\n        # Calculate prefix sums for the previous length (length - 1) to optimize summation.\n        # prev_length_prefix_sums[k] stores sum(dp[length-1][p] for p in range(1, k+1)).\n        prev_length_prefix_sums = [0] * (m + 1)\n        current_sum_for_prev_length = 0\n        for j in range(1, m + 1):\n            current_sum_for_prev_length += dp[length - 1][j]\n            prev_length_prefix_sums[j] = current_sum_for_prev_length\n\n        # For each possible last_val for the current 'length'\n        for current_val in range(1, m + 1):\n            # The previous element (prev_val) must be >= 1 and 2 * prev_val <= current_val.\n            # This implies prev_val <= current_val // 2.\n            max_prev_val_allowed = current_val // 2\n\n            # If a valid previous element can exist (max_prev_val_allowed is at least 1)\n            if max_prev_val_allowed >= 1:\n                # The number of sequences of length 'length' ending with 'current_val'\n                # is the sum of sequences of length 'length-1' ending with 'prev_val',\n                # where prev_val ranges from 1 up to max_prev_val_allowed.\n                # This sum is efficiently retrieved using the precomputed prefix sums.\n                dp[length][current_val] = prev_length_prefix_sums[max_prev_val_allowed]\n            # If max_prev_val_allowed is 0 (e.g., current_val is 1), then no valid prev_val exists,\n            # so dp[length][current_val] remains 0, which is correct.\n\n    # The total number of sequences of length n is the sum of all dp[n][j] for j from 1 to m.\n    total_sequences = 0\n    for j in range(1, m + 1):\n        total_sequences += dp[n][j]\n\n    return total_sequences"}
