{"task_id": "Mbpp/245", "solution": "def max_sum(arr: list):\n    \"\"\"Calculates the maximum sum of a bitonic subsequence in the given array.\"\"\"\n    n = len(arr)\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n    if n == 1:\n        return arr[0]\n\n    # _calculate_lis_sum calculates max sum of increasing subsequence ending at each index.\n    lis_sums = [0] * n\n    for i in range(n):\n        lis_sums[i] = arr[i]\n        for j in range(i):\n            if arr[i] > arr[j]:\n                lis_sums[i] = max(lis_sums[i], lis_sums[j] + arr[i])\n\n    # _calculate_lds_sum calculates max sum of decreasing subsequence starting at each index.\n    # It processes the array in reverse to achieve this efficiently.\n    lds_sums = [0] * n\n    for i in range(n - 1, -1, -1):\n        lds_sums[i] = arr[i]\n        for j in range(n - 1, i, -1):\n            if arr[i] > arr[j]:\n                lds_sums[i] = max(lds_sums[i], lds_sums[j] + arr[i])\n\n    max_bitonic_sum = 0\n    # A bitonic subsequence must have at least one element.\n    # We iterate through all possible peak elements (arr[i])\n    # and combine the LIS sum ending at arr[i] with the LDS sum starting at arr[i].\n    # Since arr[i] is counted twice (once in LIS and once in LDS), we subtract it.\n    for i in range(n):\n        # A bitonic subsequence requires both an increasing and decreasing part to be valid,\n        # unless it's a monotonically increasing or decreasing sequence (which are also bitonic).\n        # We ensure that lis_sums[i] and lds_sums[i] are greater than arr[i] only if they\n        # were formed by combining with at least one previous/next element respectively.\n        # However, the problem definition simply asks for \"first increasing and then decreasing\",\n        # which includes strictly increasing and strictly decreasing sequences as special cases.\n        # The current calculation (lis_sums[i] + lds_sums[i] - arr[i]) correctly covers these.\n        # A single element itself could be considered a trivial bitonic subsequence.\n        # The sum will be arr[i] if it's the only element or forms a sequence of length 1.\n        # The current DP approach inherently handles sequences of length 1 (lis_sums[i] = arr[i], lds_sums[i] = arr[i])\n        # If lis_sums[i] == arr[i] and lds_sums[i] == arr[i], then sum is arr[i].\n        \n        # To be a valid bitonic subsequence of length > 1, it must be possible to\n        # form both an increasing part and a decreasing part that meet at arr[i].\n        # If lis_sums[i] == arr[i], it means arr[i] is the smallest element in its LIS (or the LIS is just arr[i]).\n        # If lds_sums[i] == arr[i], it means arr[i] is the largest element in its LDS (or the LDS is just arr[i]).\n        \n        # A valid bitonic subsequence must be of length at least 1.\n        # If n=1, lis_sums[0] = arr[0], lds_sums[0] = arr[0].\n        # Sum = arr[0] + arr[0] - arr[0] = arr[0]. This is correct.\n        \n        # For a general array, if we have LIS of value X ending at i, and LDS of value Y starting at i,\n        # and both X > arr[i] and Y > arr[i], then it means arr[i] serves as a peak.\n        # Example: [1, 15, 51, 45, 33, 100, 12, 18, 9]\n        # At arr[i]=100 (index 5):\n        # lis_sums[5] = sum of LIS ending at 100: [1, 15, 51, 100] -> 167\n        # lds_sums[5] = sum of LDS starting at 100: [100, 18, 9] -> 127\n        # Total = 167 + 127 - 100 = 194. This requires both increasing and decreasing parts of length > 1.\n\n        # If we have a single increasing sequence like [1, 2, 3], then:\n        # lis_sums: [1, 3, 6]\n        # lds_sums: [6, 5, 3] (since [3] is LDS starting at 3, [2,3] is not valid, [1,2,3] is not valid LDS)\n        # i=0: 1+6-1 = 6 ([1,2,3])\n        # i=1: 3+5-2 = 6 ([1,2,3])\n        # i=2: 6+3-3 = 6 ([1,2,3])\n        # max = 6. This is correct.\n\n        # If we have a single decreasing sequence like [3, 2, 1]:\n        # lis_sums: [3, 2, 1]\n        # lds_sums: [6, 3, 1]\n        # i=0: 3+6-3 = 6 ([3,2,1])\n        # i=1: 2+3-2 = 3 ([3,2]) -> this is not the full sequence\n        # i=2: 1+1-1 = 1 ([1]) -> this is not the full sequence\n        # max = 6. This is correct.\n\n        # The key condition for a valid bitonic subsequence is that it must include arr[i] as the peak.\n        # If lis_sums[i] == arr[i] and lds_sums[i] == arr[i], it implies arr[i] is a standalone element.\n        # A subsequence of length 1 is trivially bitonic.\n        # The problem implies a sequence that \"is first increasing and then decreasing\", which can be length >= 1.\n        # The sum `lis_sums[i] + lds_sums[i] - arr[i]` correctly calculates the sum for a bitonic subsequence\n        # where `arr[i]` is the peak element. This handles cases where one part is empty (i.e., strictly increasing\n        # or strictly decreasing subsequences are considered bitonic).\n        \n        # A valid bitonic subsequence must have at least one element.\n        # If a bitonic subsequence has elements only from the increasing part (i.e., it's a purely increasing subsequence),\n        # its sum will be picked up when the LIS sum is combined with an LDS sum that just includes the peak element.\n        # For example, if array is [1, 2, 3], LIS ending at 3 is [1, 2, 3] (sum=6). LDS starting at 3 is [3] (sum=3).\n        # max_sum = 6 + 3 - 3 = 6. This is correct.\n        \n        # If a bitonic subsequence has elements only from the decreasing part (i.e., it's a purely decreasing subsequence),\n        # its sum will be picked up when the LDS sum is combined with an LIS sum that just includes the peak element.\n        # For example, if array is [3, 2, 1], LIS ending at 3 is [3] (sum=3). LDS starting at 3 is [3, 2, 1] (sum=6).\n        # max_sum = 3 + 6 - 3 = 6. This is correct.\n\n        # The calculation `lis_sums[i] + lds_sums[i] - arr[i]` is valid even for single-element sequences or\n        # monotonic sequences.\n        current_bitonic_sum = lis_sums[i] + lds_sums[i] - arr[i]\n        max_bitonic_sum = max(max_bitonic_sum, current_bitonic_sum)\n\n    return max_bitonic_sum"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/247", "solution": "def lps(input_string: str) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the length of the longest palindromic subsequence in the given string.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(input_string)\n\n    dp = [[0 for _ in range(n)] for _ in range(n)]\n\n    for i in range(n):\n        dp[i][i] = 1\n\n    for L in range(2, n + 1):\n        for i in range(n - L + 1):\n            j = i + L - 1\n            if input_string[i] == input_string[j] and L == 2:\n                dp[i][j] = 2\n            elif input_string[i] == input_string[j]:\n                dp[i][j] = 2 + dp[i + 1][j - 1]\n            else:\n                dp[i][j] = max(dp[i][j - 1], dp[i + 1][j])\n\n    return dp[0][n - 1]"}
