{"task_id": "Mbpp/463", "solution": "def max_subarray_product(arr: list[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the maximum product of a contiguous subarray within a given array of integers.\n    This function uses dynamic programming to keep track of the maximum and minimum products ending at the current position.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not arr:\n        # According to the problem's assert, the input array will not be empty.\n        # If an empty array were possible, a reasonable return might be 0 or raise an error.\n        # For this problem, we proceed assuming a non-empty array.\n        pass\n\n    # Initialize current_max, current_min, and overall_max with the first element of the array.\n    # current_max stores the maximum product ending at the current position.\n    # current_min stores the minimum product ending at the current position (important for negative numbers).\n    # overall_max stores the maximum product found across all subarrays so far.\n    overall_max = arr[0]\n    current_max = arr[0]\n    current_min = arr[0]\n\n    # Iterate through the array starting from the second element.\n    for num in arr[1:]:\n        # If the current number is negative, swapping current_max and current_min\n        # helps handle the fact that multiplying by a negative number flips their roles.\n        # The previously smallest negative product could become the largest positive product.\n        if num < 0:\n            current_max, current_min = current_min, current_max\n\n        # Update current_max: it's either the number itself, or the number multiplied by the previous current_max.\n        current_max = max(num, current_max * num)\n        # Update current_min: it's either the number itself, or the number multiplied by the previous current_min.\n        current_min = min(num, current_min * num)\n\n        # Update overall_max with the maximum product found so far.\n        overall_max = max(overall_max, current_max)\n\n    return overall_max"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/470", "solution": "def add_pairwise(input_tuple: tuple) -> tuple:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the sum of adjacent elements in an input tuple.\n    Returns a new tuple with these sums.\n    \"\"\"\n    if len(input_tuple) < 2:\n        return ()\n\n    result = []\n    for i in range(len(input_tuple) - 1):\n        result.append(input_tuple[i] + input_tuple[i+1])\n    return tuple(result)"}
