{"task_id": "Mbpp/472", "solution": "def check_Consecutive(numbers: list) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if the given list of numbers contains consecutive numbers.\n    A list is considered consecutive if, when sorted, each number is exactly one greater\n    than the preceding number.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not numbers:\n        return True\n\n    sorted_numbers = sorted(numbers)\n    for i in range(1, len(sorted_numbers)):\n        if sorted_numbers[i] != sorted_numbers[i-1] + 1:\n            return False\n    return True"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/468", "solution": "def max_product(arr: list[int | float]) -> int | float:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the maximum product of an increasing subsequence in the given array.\n\n    Args:\n        arr: A list of numbers (integers or floats).\n\n    Returns:\n        The maximum product found, or 0 if the array is empty.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not arr:\n        return 0\n\n    # Initialize dp_products array where dp_products[i] stores the maximum product\n    # of an increasing subsequence ending at index i.\n    # Each element can be an increasing subsequence of length 1, so initialize with arr[i].\n    dp_products = list(arr)\n\n    # Iterate through the array to build up dp_products\n    for i in range(len(arr)):\n        # For each element arr[i], check previous elements arr[j]\n        for j in range(i):\n            # If arr[i] can extend an increasing subsequence ending at arr[j]\n            # and the product ending at j is positive (as per design guidance for max positive product)\n            if arr[i] > arr[j] and dp_products[j] > 0:\n                potential_product = dp_products[j] * arr[i]\n                # Update dp_products[i] if a larger product is found\n                dp_products[i] = max(dp_products[i], potential_product)\n\n    # The maximum value within dp_products will be the overall maximum product\n    return max(dp_products)"}
